    ====================================
    Dark Oberon README
    ====================================

Dark oberon is a real-time strategy released under GPL.

Homepage: http://dark-oberon.sourceforge.net/

Original Authors

Valeria Sventova    <liberty@matfyz.cz>
Jiri Krejsa         <crazych@matfyz.cz>
Peter Knut          <peterpp@matfyz.cz>
Martin Kosalko      <cauchy@matfyz.cz>
Marian Cerny        <jojo@matfyz.cz>
Michal Kral         <index@matfyz.cz>

This fork modernises the game for current operating systems (SDL2 instead of
GLFW 2 / FMOD) and extends it: an orc race, a smarter CPU AI, a map generator
and a dedicated headless server. The build in this tree is maintained for
Linux; see section 4 for ways to run or compile on Windows. The version lives
in the VERSION file; changes are listed in CHANGELOG.md.

Content
=======

  1. Requirements
  2. How to compile
  3. How to run
  4. Windows (build / run)

1. Requirements
===============

Hardware requirements
---------------------

  o Athlon 1 GHz (or equivalent Pentium)
  o graphics card with 3D support (OpenGL)
  o 512 MB RAM

Software requirements
---------------------

  UNIX-like OS for the default build (Linux is what this fork is tested on;
  Windows options are described in section 4).

  Additional libraries required (Linux / current tree):

    o SDL2 — window, input, timing (replaces GLFW 2)
    o OpenGL + GLU (Mesa or vendor drivers)
    o SDL2_mixer — only if you build with sound (make SOUND=1); replaces FMOD

2. How to compile
=================

   From the repository root run

       make

   (or enter src/ and run make). The binary dark-oberon is written to the
   repository root. The dedicated headless server is built with
   `make -C src server` (see server/ for the Docker setup).

   For sound and music, build from src/ with:

       make SOUND=1

   You need SDL2_mixer and its codec dependencies.

   If the linker cannot find -lSDL2 or -lSDL2_mixer, install the development
   packages for SDL2 / SDL2_mixer or adjust src/Makefile (SDL2_LIBS /
   SDL2_MIXER_LIBS).

   If you had any troubles with compiling, the original project help forum is
   linked from the homepage above.

3. How to run
=============

  You can run Dark Oberon with:

    ./dark-oberon

4. Windows (build / run)
========================

  This repository's `src/Makefile` targets a typical Linux toolchain: GNU make,
  `pkg-config` (or `sdl2-config`), and `-lGL` / `-lGLU`. It is not regularly
  built on Windows here, but you still have several options.

  A) Run the Linux build under WSL2 (most practical for this fork)
  ----------------------------------------------------------------

  Install a Linux distro in WSL2, then follow sections 1–3 inside that
  environment (same packages as on native Linux). You need a GUI path from WSL to Windows:

    o Windows 11: WSLg often works out of the box for OpenGL/SDL apps.
    o Otherwise: an X server on Windows (e.g. VcXsrv, X410) and correct
      `DISPLAY`, or another supported graphics forwarding setup.

  B) Use a legacy native Windows binary (original stack)
  ------------------------------------------------------

  The upstream project shipped Windows builds (older GLFW + FMOD stack), not
  SDL2. The game homepage / SourceForge release may still provide them. A copy
  of such a tree (including `dark-oberon.exe` and `dat/`) may also be kept
  locally for reference only — it is not the same code path as this SDL2 fork.

  C) Native compile on Windows (MSYS2 / MinGW-w64) — manual / unsupported
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------

  In principle you can install MSYS2, then the `mingw-w64-x86_64-gcc`,
  `mingw-w64-x86_64-SDL2`, and (for sound) `mingw-w64-x86_64-SDL2_mixer`
  packages, and try to compile from `src/` with a Makefile adjusted for Windows:

    o Replace Linux GL link flags with MinGW equivalents, typically
      `-lopengl32 -lglu32` instead of `-lGL -lGLU`.
    o If `pkg-config` is unavailable in your shell, set `SDL2_CFLAGS` and
      `SDL2_LIBS` (and mixer variables) by hand to match your install paths.
    o Keep `-DUNIX=1` (as in the current Makefile) so the code uses the
      POSIX-style networking paths that MinGW’s environment provides; do not
      switch to `-DWINDOWS` unless you port the build to MSVC and Winsock.

  There is no checked-in Windows project file; expect to maintain local Makefile
  overrides or a small wrapper Makefile. Patches that add a clean, tested
  Windows/MinGW target are welcome.
